  "name": "ASCA (ANTI SACCHAROMYCES CEREVISIASE ABTIBODIES) - IgA",
  "code": "2683",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table style=\"width: 100.065%;\" border=\"1\"><colgroup><col style=\"width: 49.9303%;\"><col style=\"width: 49.9303%;\"></colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>RESULT IN UNITS&nbsp;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>REMARKS</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0-20</td>\n<td>Negative result indicating no ASCA antibody detected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20.1-24.9&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Equivocal result. Retesting advised.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt;=25&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Positive result indicating the presence of ASCA antibody. Possibility of Crohn&rsquo;s disease is likely.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>&nbsp;Note</strong><br>1.False positivity is seen due to the presence of Immune complexes or other immunoglobulin aggregates in sample<br>2.Negative ASCA result does not rule out the possibility of Crohn&rsquo;s disease<br>3.Presence of both IgG &amp; IgA ASCA antibodies increases the specificity to nearly 100% for Crohn&rsquo;s disease<br><br><strong>Comments</strong><br>Most common forms of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD) are Ulcerative colitis and Crohn&rsquo;s disease both of which produce inflammation of small and large intestines. Crohn&rsquo;s disease affects the entire digestive tract producing ulcers that extend deep into the intestinal wall. Patients with IBD have shown presence of antibodies ( ASCA &amp; ANCA) in serum that help to distinguish between Ulcerative colitis &amp; Crohn&rsquo;s disease. ASCA antibodies are more prevalent in Crohn&rsquo;s disease as compared to Ulcerative colitis which shows presence of Neutrophil specific antibodies (ANCA). Approximately 55% patients with Crohn&rsquo;s disease have measurable IgA / IgG ASCA antibodies that react with cell wall of Saccharomyces cerevisiae&nbsp;strain. Presence of both IgG &amp; IgA ASCA antibodies increases the specificity to nearly 100% for Crohn&rsquo;s disease.</p>",
